+#### Laplacian Smooth Loss
+
+### definition
+- [S1] A regularization term to reduce mesh distortion (e.g., intersecting triangles) by minimizing the distance between each vertex and the average location of its neighbors.
+
+### formula
+- [S1] $L_{lap} = \frac{1}{|V|} \sum_{i \in V} \| v_i - \frac{1}{|N(i)|} \sum_{j \in N(i)} v_j \|$, where $V$ is the set of vertices, $v_i$ is a vertex, $N(i)$ is the set of neighbor vertices of $v_i$, and $|V|$ is the total number of vertices.
+
+### parameter
+- [S1] $\lambda_{lap}$: The weight of the Laplacian smooth loss in the total training objective.
 
 ## Background
-The standard Marching Cubes algorithm [Lookup Table and Ambiguity Handling](10327-lookup-table-and-ambiguity-handling.md) produces meshes with vertices constrained to grid edges, leading to geometric errors. Subsequent research focused on decoupling vertex position from the linear interpolation constraint. QEF-based methods [3831-marching-cubes-with-qef-vertex-optimization.md] adapt the Point Set Surfaces approach to Marching Cubes. Position Error Minimization [7435-position-error-minimized-marching-cubes.md] offers a lighter-weight alternative. Edge Transformations [10929-edge-transformations-for-improving-mesh-quality-of-marching-.md] focus on mesh quality metrics. Midpoint Marching Cubes [12551-midpoint-marching-cubes.md] represents an early simplification for specific hardware constraints.
+The concept of optimizing vertex positions to improve mesh quality or fit underlying data is a general technique in computer graphics. Specific applications to Marching Cubes include QEF-based optimization for handling ambiguities [S1], position error minimization for snapping vertices to the isosurface [S1], and edge transformations for improving triangle aspect ratios [S1]. Laplacian smoothing is a standard regularization technique used in mesh optimization to prevent distortion during iterative refinement processes [S1].
 
